from extras import ex

ex('investigate-bounce',"Drop the ball from different heights and measure the bounce height with a metre ruler held upright. Look at the bottom of the ball at eye level to avoid parallax error. The bounce height is usually less than the drop height.",
 [('Why does a ball not bounce back to its starting height?','Some energy is transferred to heating and sound.','Energy is destroyed.','Gravity switches off.','Energy is dissipated to the surroundings.'),
  ('How can you measure bounce height more accurately?','Film it in slow motion next to a ruler.','Guess by eye from far away.','Use a bathroom scale.','Video lets you pause at the top.'),
  ('Which is the independent variable if you change the drop height?','Drop height.','Bounce height.','Type of floor.','You choose the drop height.'),
  ('Reading the ruler at eye level avoids…','parallax error.','random error from temperature.','anomalies in mass.','Viewing at an angle misreads the scale.')],
 [('Which energy store decreases as the ball falls?','Gravitational potential energy store.'),
  ('A ball is dropped from 100 cm and bounces to 60 cm. Calculate the bounce height as a percentage of the drop height.','60 ÷ 100 = 0.6.~0.6 × 100 = 60%.'),
  ('Explain why a ball bounces lower on carpet than on a hard floor.','Carpet squashes more when the ball lands.~More energy is transferred to the thermal store of the carpet (and sound).~So less energy is returned to the ball’s kinetic and gravitational stores.')])

ex('investigate-bungee',"Add equal masses one at a time and measure the length each time. Calculate the extension by subtracting the original length. Plot load against extension to see the pattern.",
 [('A cord is 20 cm long and stretches to 26 cm. What is the extension?','6 cm.','26 cm.','46 cm.','26 − 20 = 6 cm.'),
  ('What is the independent variable?','The load (weight) added.','The extension.','The colour of the cord.','You choose the load.'),
  ('Why should you wear goggles?','The cord could snap and flick up.','To see the ruler better.','Because of chemicals.','A stretched cord stores energy.'),
  ('If the cord does not return to its original length, it has been…','stretched beyond its elastic limit.','measured wrongly.','heated.','It is permanently deformed.')],
 [('What is extension?','The extra length when something is stretched (stretched length − original length).'),
  ('A cord’s extension is 4 cm with 2 N. Predict the extension with 4 N if it stretches evenly, and explain.','8 cm.~Doubling the force doubles the extension (they are proportional).'),
  ('Explain why the bungee cord in a real bungee jump must be tested carefully.','The cord must stretch enough to slow the jumper gently.~It must not stretch so far that the jumper hits the ground.~It must not go past its elastic limit or snap.')])

ex('investigate-conduction',"Fix rods of different metals to a heat source with wax or a temperature sensor at the far end. Time how long it takes for the wax to melt. The rods must be the same length and thickness.",
 [('Which metal is usually the best thermal conductor?','Copper.','Iron.','Steel.','Copper conducts heat very well.'),
  ('Why must all the rods be the same thickness?','To make it a fair test.','So they are heavier.','So they melt.','Thickness affects heat transfer.'),
  ('What is the dependent variable?','Time for the wax to melt.','Type of metal.','Length of rod.','You measure the time.'),
  ('Why are saucepan handles made of plastic or wood?','They are poor conductors (insulators).','They are good conductors.','They are cheap metals.','They stop heat reaching your hand.')],
 [('What is conduction?','Transfer of energy through a material by vibrating particles.'),
  ('Wax melts after 30 s on copper and 90 s on steel. Which is the better conductor and why?','Copper.~Energy reached the end faster, so the wax melted sooner.'),
  ('Explain how energy is transferred along a metal rod.','Particles at the hot end vibrate more.~They pass energy to neighbouring particles as they collide.~In metals, free electrons also carry energy quickly along the rod.')])

ex('investigate-cupcake',"Drop paper cases from the same height and time the fall. Stack cases to change the mass without changing the shape. Heavier stacks usually reach a higher terminal velocity and land sooner.",
 [('When a falling case reaches terminal velocity, the forces are…','balanced.','unbalanced downwards.','zero in total because weight disappears.','Air resistance equals weight.'),
  ('Why stack cases to increase the mass?','The shape stays the same, so it is a fair test.','To make them bounce.','So they fall slower.','Only mass changes.'),
  ('Air resistance increases when the case…','moves faster.','stops.','gets lighter.','Faster objects hit more air particles.'),
  ('Which forces act on a falling paper case?','Weight down and air resistance up.','Friction and thrust.','Upthrust and magnetism.','Two main forces act.')],
 [('What is terminal velocity?','The steady top speed reached when air resistance equals weight.'),
  ('Describe how the speed of a dropped paper case changes as it falls.','It speeds up at first because weight is greater than air resistance.~Air resistance increases until it equals weight, then it falls at a steady speed.'),
  ('Explain why a stack of four cases lands sooner than one case.','The stack has more weight but the same shape.~It must fall faster before air resistance balances its weight.~So its terminal velocity is higher and it lands sooner.')])

ex('investigate-salt',"Measure the time for a fixed mass of salt to dissolve in water at different temperatures. Stir the same way each time, or not at all. Warmer water usually dissolves salt faster.",
 [('What is the independent variable if you change water temperature?','Temperature of the water.','Dissolving time.','Mass of salt.','You choose the temperature.'),
  ('Why does salt dissolve faster in warm water?','Water particles move faster and break up the solid more quickly.','Warm water is heavier.','Salt melts.','Faster particles collide more.'),
  ('Which is a control variable?','Mass of salt.','Dissolving time.','Water temperature.','Keep it the same.'),
  ('Stirring makes salt dissolve…','faster.','slower.','not at all.','Stirring brings fresh water to the salt.')],
 [('What does soluble mean?','Able to dissolve in a liquid.'),
  ('Times to dissolve: 20 °C = 60 s, 40 °C = 35 s, 60 °C = 20 s. Describe the pattern.','As temperature increases, the dissolving time decreases.~So salt dissolves faster in warmer water.'),
  ('Explain why it is hard to decide exactly when the salt has finished dissolving, and how to improve this.','It is difficult to see the last tiny grains.~Different people judge the end point differently.~Use a black background/light or a conductivity meter to judge the end point consistently.')])

ex('investigate-diffusion',"Add one drop of food colouring to still water at different temperatures and time how long it takes to spread evenly. Do not stir or move the beaker. Warmer water spreads colour faster.",
 [('Why does colour spread faster in warm water?','The particles move faster.','Warm water is a different colour.','Particles get bigger.','Faster particles mix more quickly.'),
  ('Why should you not stir the water?','Stirring would mix it, not diffusion.','Stirring cools it.','It is unsafe.','You want to measure diffusion only.'),
  ('What is the dependent variable?','Time for the colour to spread evenly.','Water temperature.','Size of beaker.','You measure the time.'),
  ('Diffusion happens from…','high concentration to low concentration.','low to high concentration.','top to bottom only.','Particles spread out.')],
 [('What is diffusion?','The spreading of particles from high to low concentration.'),
  ('Give two control variables in the food colouring investigation.','Volume of water / size of beaker.~Number of drops / type of food colouring / no stirring.'),
  ('Explain, using particles, why the colour spreads out even though nobody stirs the water.','All particles in a liquid are always moving randomly.~Colour particles collide with water particles and move in all directions.~Overall they spread from where there are many to where there are few.')])

ex('magnet',"Magnetic materials include iron, nickel and cobalt. Field lines go from the north pole to the south pole, and lines close together show a stronger field. A compass needle is a small magnet that points along the field.",
 [('Which metal is magnetic?','Iron.','Copper.','Aluminium.','Iron, nickel and cobalt are magnetic.'),
  ('Two north poles placed together will…','repel.','attract.','do nothing.','Like poles repel.'),
  ('Magnetic field lines point from…','north to south.','south to north.','the middle outwards only.','By convention, N to S outside the magnet.'),
  ('Where is a bar magnet’s field strongest?','At the poles.','In the middle.','Far away from it.','Field lines are closest at the poles.')],
 [('Name a magnetic material.','Iron / nickel / cobalt / steel.'),
  ('Describe how to show the shape of a bar magnet’s field.','Place a plotting compass near the magnet and mark where the needle points.~Move it along and join the marks with lines (or sprinkle iron filings on paper over the magnet).'),
  ('Explain why a compass needle points north on Earth.','The needle is a small magnet.~Earth has a magnetic field, like a giant bar magnet.~The needle lines up with Earth’s field, pointing towards the magnetic pole in the north.')])

ex('electromagnet',"Electromagnets can be turned on and off, which is why they are used in scrapyard cranes, door locks and electric bells. Reversing the current reverses the poles. The field of a coil looks like a bar magnet's field.",
 [('Give one way to make an electromagnet stronger.','Add more turns of wire.','Use a plastic core.','Reduce the current.','More coils give a stronger field.'),
  ('Why are electromagnets used in scrapyard cranes?','They can be switched off to drop the metal.','They are permanent magnets.','They do not need electricity.','Switching off releases the load.'),
  ('Reversing the current in an electromagnet…','swaps the north and south poles.','makes it stronger.','turns it into copper.','Field direction depends on current direction.'),
  ('Which core makes an electromagnet strongest?','Iron.','Wood.','Plastic.','Iron is magnetic and concentrates the field.')],
 [('What is an electromagnet?','A coil of wire that becomes magnetic when a current flows through it.'),
  ('Give two ways to increase the strength of an electromagnet.','More turns (coils) of wire.~A larger current / an iron core.'),
  ('Explain why an electromagnet is more useful than a permanent magnet in an electric door lock.','The electromagnet can be turned on and off with a switch.~When on it holds the door locked; when off it releases.~A permanent magnet cannot be switched off.')])

ex('work',"Simple machines such as levers, ramps and pulleys make jobs easier by reducing the force needed. You must move the force through a bigger distance, so the work done is not reduced. 1 joule of work is done when a 1 N force moves 1 m.",
 [('A 50 N force pushes a box 4 m. How much work is done?','200 J.','54 J.','12.5 J.','W = F × d = 50 × 4.'),
  ('What is the unit of work done?','Joule (J).','Newton (N).','Watt (W).','Work is energy transferred.'),
  ('A ramp lets you lift a load using…','a smaller force over a longer distance.','a bigger force over a shorter distance.','no force.','Work done is about the same.'),
  ('No work is done if…','the object does not move.','the force is large.','you push horizontally.','Distance moved is zero.')],
 [('Write the equation for work done.','Work done = force × distance moved (in the direction of the force).'),
  ('A crane lifts a 2000 N load through 15 m. Calculate the work done.','W = 2000 × 15.~= 30 000 J.'),
  ('Explain why pushing a heavy box up a ramp is easier than lifting it straight up, even though the work done is about the same.','The ramp means you push the box over a longer distance.~So a smaller force is needed.~Work = force × distance, so a smaller force over a longer distance transfers about the same energy.')])

ex('moments',"A lever can multiply force: a small force far from the pivot can balance a large force close to the pivot. When a seesaw balances, clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments. Moment is measured in newton metres, N m.",
 [('A 20 N force acts 3 m from a pivot. What is the moment?','60 N m.','23 N m.','6.7 N m.','M = F × d.'),
  ('What is the unit of moment?','Newton metre (N m).','Newton (N).','Joule only.','Force × distance.'),
  ('Why is a long spanner easier to use?','The bigger distance gives a bigger moment for the same force.','It is heavier.','It makes the nut smaller.','Moment = F × d.'),
  ('A seesaw balances when…','clockwise moments equal anticlockwise moments.','both people weigh the same, always.','the pivot moves.','This is the principle of moments.')],
 [('What is a pivot?','The point that an object turns about.'),
  ('A 300 N child sits 2 m from the pivot of a seesaw. How far from the pivot must a 400 N child sit on the other side to balance?','300 × 2 = 600 N m.~600 ÷ 400 = 1.5 m.'),
  ('Explain why a door handle is placed far from the hinges.','The hinge is the pivot.~A bigger distance from the pivot gives a bigger moment for the same force.~So less force is needed to open the door.')])

ex('thermal',"Temperature is measured in degrees Celsius with a thermometer. The energy needed to heat something depends on its mass, the material and the temperature rise. Energy always flows from hot to cold until they reach the same temperature.",
 [('Energy flows from a hot cup of tea to…','the cooler surroundings.','the hotter surroundings.','nowhere.','Heat flows from hot to cold.'),
  ('A bath of warm water has more thermal energy than a cup of boiling water because…','it has much more mass.','it is hotter.','it is a different liquid.','Thermal energy depends on mass too.'),
  ('Which instrument measures temperature?','Thermometer.','Newtonmeter.','Ammeter.','Temperature is in °C.'),
  ('Two objects at the same temperature…','have no net energy transfer between them.','always transfer energy.','are both cold.','This is thermal equilibrium.')],
 [('What is the unit of temperature in school science?','Degrees Celsius (°C).'),
  ('A drink cools from 70 °C to 25 °C in a 25 °C room. Describe what happens and why it stops cooling.','Energy transfers from the drink to the cooler room.~It stops when the drink reaches room temperature (25 °C).'),
  ('Explain why it takes longer to boil a full kettle than a half-full kettle.','A full kettle has a larger mass of water.~More energy is needed to raise its temperature by the same amount.~The kettle transfers energy at the same rate, so it takes longer.')])

ex('convection',"Shiny surfaces reflect infrared and dull black surfaces absorb and emit it best. Insulators such as foam, wool and trapped air reduce conduction and convection. Houses lose less energy with loft insulation, cavity wall insulation and double glazing.",
 [('Which surface is the best emitter of infrared?','Dull black.','Shiny silver.','White gloss.','Dark matt surfaces emit and absorb best.'),
  ('Why is warm air in a room found near the ceiling?','Warm air is less dense, so it rises.','Warm air is heavier.','Ceilings attract heat.','This causes convection currents.'),
  ('Loft insulation works mainly by…','trapping air, which is a poor conductor.','reflecting sound.','absorbing water.','Trapped air reduces conduction and convection.'),
  ('Which energy transfer can happen through a vacuum?','Infrared radiation.','Conduction.','Convection.','Radiation does not need particles.')],
 [('What is convection?','Energy transfer by a warm liquid or gas rising and cooler fluid sinking (a current).'),
  ('Give two ways to reduce energy loss from a house.','Loft insulation / cavity wall insulation.~Double glazing / draught excluders / carpets.'),
  ('Explain why a radiator heats the whole room even though it is only on one wall.','Air next to the radiator is heated and becomes less dense.~The warm air rises and cooler air moves in to replace it.~This sets up a convection current that carries energy around the room.')])

ex('atmosphere',"Air is about 78% nitrogen and 21% oxygen. Carbon moves between the air, living things and the oceans in the carbon cycle. Photosynthesis removes carbon dioxide; respiration, decay and burning release it.",
 [('Which gas makes up most of the air?','Nitrogen.','Oxygen.','Carbon dioxide.','About 78% nitrogen.'),
  ('Which process removes carbon dioxide from the air?','Photosynthesis.','Respiration.','Burning fossil fuels.','Plants take in CO₂.'),
  ('About what percentage of air is oxygen?','21%.','78%.','1%.','About one fifth.'),
  ('Which process releases carbon dioxide?','Decay by microorganisms.','Photosynthesis.','Dissolving in rocks only.','Decomposers respire.')],
 [('Name the process by which plants take carbon dioxide out of the air.','Photosynthesis.'),
  ('Give two processes that add carbon dioxide to the atmosphere.','Respiration / decay.~Burning (combustion) of fossil fuels or wood.'),
  ('Explain how cutting down and burning forests affects the amount of carbon dioxide in the air.','Burning the trees releases carbon dioxide.~Fewer trees means less photosynthesis.~So less carbon dioxide is removed and its level in the air rises.')])

ex('climate',"Burning fossil fuels and cutting down forests increase carbon dioxide levels. More greenhouse gases trap more energy, raising global temperatures. Effects include melting ice, rising sea levels and more extreme weather.",
 [('Which is a greenhouse gas?','Carbon dioxide.','Nitrogen.','Oxygen.','CO₂ absorbs infrared radiation.'),
  ('What is a main cause of increasing carbon dioxide levels?','Burning fossil fuels.','Planting trees.','Using solar panels.','Combustion releases CO₂.'),
  ('Which is an effect of global warming?','Rising sea levels.','More ice at the poles.','Colder summers everywhere.','Ice melts and water expands.'),
  ('Weather is short-term; climate is…','the average weather over many years.','tomorrow’s forecast.','one storm.','Climate is long-term.')],
 [('What is the greenhouse effect?','Gases in the atmosphere trapping energy and keeping Earth warm.'),
  ('Give two effects of climate change.','Rising sea levels / melting ice caps.~More extreme weather / changes in where plants and animals can live.'),
  ('Explain how more carbon dioxide in the atmosphere leads to higher temperatures.','The Earth emits infrared radiation.~Carbon dioxide absorbs it and sends some back towards the Earth.~More carbon dioxide traps more energy, so the average temperature rises.')])

ex('extraction',"Metals less reactive than carbon, such as iron, are extracted by heating the ore with carbon. Very reactive metals such as aluminium are extracted by electrolysis, which uses a lot of electricity. Gold is found as the pure metal because it is so unreactive.",
 [('How is iron extracted from its ore?','Heating with carbon.','Electrolysis.','Dissolving in water.','Carbon is more reactive than iron.'),
  ('Why is gold found as the pure metal?','It is very unreactive.','It is very reactive.','It is magnetic.','It does not combine with oxygen.'),
  ('Aluminium is extracted by electrolysis because…','it is more reactive than carbon.','it is less reactive than carbon.','it is a gas.','Carbon cannot take its oxygen.'),
  ('What is an ore?','A rock containing enough metal to be worth extracting.','A pure metal.','A type of fuel.','Ores contain metal compounds.')],
 [('What is an ore?','A rock containing enough metal (compound) to make extraction worthwhile.'),
  ('Complete the word equation: iron oxide + carbon → … + …','Iron.~Carbon dioxide.'),
  ('Explain why copper can be extracted with carbon but aluminium cannot.','Carbon is more reactive than copper, so it can remove the oxygen from copper oxide.~Aluminium is more reactive than carbon.~So carbon cannot displace it, and electrolysis must be used.')])

ex('recycle',"Recycling metals uses less energy than extracting them from ores. It saves ores, which are finite, and reduces mining damage and landfill. Collecting and sorting waste does cost money and energy.",
 [('Why is recycling aluminium cans useful?','It uses much less energy than extracting new aluminium.','It makes cans heavier.','It makes new ore.','Electrolysis uses lots of electricity.'),
  ('Metal ores are a finite resource, which means they…','will run out.','are renewable.','grow back.','There is a limited amount.'),
  ('A disadvantage of recycling is that…','collecting and sorting waste costs money and energy.','it uses more ore.','it makes more landfill.','Transport and sorting are needed.'),
  ('Which is reusing rather than recycling?','Refilling a glass bottle.','Melting cans to make new cans.','Crushing cars for steel.','Reuse keeps the item as it is.')],
 [('What is recycling?','Processing waste materials to make new products.'),
  ('Give two advantages of recycling steel.','Saves energy compared with extracting iron from ore.~Saves iron ore / reduces mining / reduces landfill.'),
  ('Explain why recycling aluminium is especially good for the environment.','Extracting aluminium needs electrolysis, which uses a lot of electricity.~Recycling uses only about 5% of that energy.~Less electricity means less fossil fuel burned and less carbon dioxide released.')])

ex('selection',"Charles Darwin developed the theory of evolution by natural selection. Bacteria can become resistant to antibiotics by natural selection. Over many generations, a species changes as helpful features become more common.",
 [('Who proposed the theory of evolution by natural selection?','Charles Darwin.','Isaac Newton.','Marie Curie.','Darwin published in 1859.'),
  ('In natural selection, which individuals are most likely to survive?','Those best adapted to the environment.','The largest ones always.','The youngest ones.','Helpful variations aid survival.'),
  ('How do bacteria become resistant to antibiotics?','Resistant bacteria survive and reproduce.','Antibiotics teach bacteria.','Bacteria become larger.','Non-resistant ones are killed.'),
  ('Why do helpful features become more common over time?','Survivors pass on their genes to offspring.','Animals choose to change.','The environment creates new genes on purpose.','Inherited features are passed on.')],
 [('What is natural selection?','The process where individuals best suited to their environment survive and reproduce, passing on their genes.'),
  ('Give the two main steps after variation in natural selection.','Individuals with helpful features are more likely to survive (and reproduce).~They pass on their genes, so the feature becomes more common.'),
  ('Explain how peppered moths became darker in polluted areas.','There was variation in colour; dark moths were camouflaged on sooty trees.~Birds ate more pale moths, so more dark moths survived and reproduced.~Dark moths passed on the gene, so dark moths became more common.')])
